    answer="An ice dam is a ridge of ice at the roof's edge that traps melting snow and forces water under shingles and into the house. To deal with one safely, pull snow off the roof from the ground with a roof rake, run a fan and cool the attic from inside, and call a professional for steam removal rather than chipping the ice. Professional removal commonly costs a few hundred to a couple of thousand dollars. Standard homeowners insurance usually covers sudden interior water damage from an ice dam, but not wear or neglect, and not the cost of fixing the insulation and ventilation that caused it. The lasting fix is air sealing, insulation and ventilation in the attic.",
